    # Scrape Booking.com hotels.
    #
    # Searches Booking.com for `location` and returns the properties as JSON: `name`, `price`
    # (with `checkin`/`checkout`), `reviewScore`, `address`, coordinates, `image` and a hotel
    # `url`. Set `adults`, `children`, `rooms` and `currency`, and collect up to `max_results`
    # across pages. Prices only appear when check-in/check-out dates are given.
    #
    # @param location [String] City or place to search, e.g. 'Austin' or 'Paris'.
    # @param checkin [String] Check-in YYYY-MM-DD (needed for prices).
    # @param checkout [String] Check-out YYYY-MM-DD.
    # @param adults [Integer] Number of adults.
    # @param children [Integer] Number of children.
    # @param rooms [Integer] Number of rooms.
    # @param currency [String] Price currency: USD, EUR, GBP, AUD, CAD, CHF, JPY, CNY, INR, BRL,
    # MXN, PLN, SEK, NOK, DKK, AED, SGD, ZAR, TRY, RUB. API default: USD.
    # @param max_results [Integer] How many properties to collect across pages. API default: 25.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE').
    # Calls POST /travel/booking.
    def booking_hotels(location, checkin: nil, checkout: nil, adults: nil, children: nil, rooms: nil, currency: nil, max_results: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/travel/booking", {
        location: location,
        checkin: checkin,
        checkout: checkout,
        adults: adults,
        children: children,
        rooms: rooms,
        currency: currency,
        max_results: max_results,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape Booking.com hotel reviews.
    #
    # Given a Booking.com `hotel` (page URL or `<cc>/<slug>`), returns its newest guest
    # `reviews`: `id`, `reviewDate`, `rating`, `ratingText`, `title`, `ratingBand`,
    # `positiveText`, `negativeText`, `reviewer` (`name`, `country`, `countryCode`), `tripType`,
    # `travelerType`, `nights`, `stayMonth`, `helpfulVotes`, `submittedFromMobile`, `language`
    # and the raw `tags`. Booking serves the newest 15 reviews written in each language: pick
    # the languages with `languages` (or `all`), cap the total with `max_results` and drop older
    # reviews with `since`. Billed one unit per language page fetched (`pagesFetched`).
    #
    # @param hotel [String] Booking.com hotel page URL, e.g.
    # https://www.booking.com/hotel/fr/le-meurice.html (a locale suffix such as .en-gb.html and
    # query parameters are fine), or `<cc>/<slug>` such as fr/le-meurice.
    # @param languages [String] Review languages to read, comma-separated, or `all`: en, de, fr,
    # es, it, nl, pt, pl, ru, ja, zh, ko, sv, da, no, fi, cs, tr. Default en. Booking serves the
    # newest 15 reviews written in each language; each language is one page and one billed unit,
    # fetched in the order given until max_results is reached.
    # @param max_results [Integer] How many reviews to return across languages (1-270, at most
    # 15 per language). API default: 15.
    # @param since [String] Only reviews written on or after this date (YYYY-MM-DD).
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE').
    # Calls POST /travel/booking-reviews.
    def booking_reviews(hotel, languages: nil, max_results: nil, since: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/travel/booking-reviews", {
        hotel: hotel,
        languages: languages,
        max_results: max_results,
        since: since,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape Airbnb stay listings.
    #
    # Searches Airbnb for `location` and returns the stay listings as JSON: `name`, `roomType`,
    # `price` (USD by default) with any original price, `rating` and `reviewCount`, coordinates,
    # `badges` (e.g. Guest favourite), `image` and a clean room `url`. Filter with
    # `checkin`/`checkout`, `adults`, `min_price`/`max_price` and `room_type`, and collect up to
    # `max_results` across pages.
    #
    # @param location [String] City to search, e.g. 'Austin, TX' or 'Paris, France'.
    # @param checkin [String] Check-in date YYYY-MM-DD (with checkout).
    # @param checkout [String] Check-out date YYYY-MM-DD (with checkin).
    # @param adults [Integer] Number of adult guests.
    # @param room_type [String] Room type filter: entire_home, private_room, hotel_room,
    # shared_room. Omit for all.
    # @param min_price [Integer] Minimum nightly price.
    # @param max_price [Integer] Maximum nightly price.
    # @param max_results [Integer] How many listings to collect across pages. API default: 48.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE'). Defaults to US so prices are in USD.
    # Calls POST /travel/airbnb.
    def airbnb_search(location, checkin: nil, checkout: nil, adults: nil, room_type: nil, min_price: nil, max_price: nil, max_results: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/travel/airbnb", {
        location: location,
        checkin: checkin,
        checkout: checkout,
        adults: adults,
        room_type: room_type,
        min_price: min_price,
        max_price: max_price,
        max_results: max_results,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape an Airbnb listing's full details.
    #
    # Fetches one Airbnb stay (by listing id or room URL) and returns it as JSON: `title`,
    # `description`, `roomType`, `propertyType`, `location` (city, country, `lat`/`lng`), `host`
    # (name, `isSuperhost`, `responseRate`, `yearsHosting`, photo), `rating` (overall plus
    # accuracy, cleanliness, check-in, communication, location, value), `reviewsCount`,
    # `guests`, `bedrooms`, `beds`, `bathrooms`, grouped `amenities`, `photos` with captions,
    # `highlights`, `houseRules`, `checkIn`/`checkOut` times and `isGuestFavorite`. Add
    # `check_in`/`check_out` for `available`, the `cancellationPolicy` and `price` (total,
    # nightly, breakdown) in `currency`; add `include_reviews` for up to `max_reviews` guest
    # `reviews`. Companion to the Airbnb search plugin (`/travel/airbnb`).
    #
    # @param room [String] Airbnb listing id (e.g. 23860926) or a room URL on any Airbnb domain,
    # e.g. 'https://www.airbnb.co.uk/rooms/23860926'.
    # @param check_in [String] Check-in date YYYY-MM-DD. With check_out, adds price,
    # availability and the cancellation policy.
    # @param check_out [String] Check-out date YYYY-MM-DD (with check_in).
    # @param adults [Integer] Adult guests (affects the price). API default: 1.
    # @param children [Integer] Children aged 2-12. API default: 0.
    # @param infants [Integer] Infants under 2. API default: 0.
    # @param pets [Integer] Pets. API default: 0.
    # @param currency [String] Price currency, ISO code such as USD, EUR, GBP. API default: USD.
    # @param locale [String] Language for Airbnb's texts, e.g. en, de, fr, es, pt-BR. Host-
    # written text is returned translated when Airbnb has a translation. API default: en.
    # @param include_reviews [Boolean] Also return guest reviews. API default: false.
    # @param max_reviews [Integer] How many reviews to return with include_reviews (1-100). API
    # default: 20.
    # @param reviews_sort [String] Review order: most_relevant, most_recent, highest_rated,
    # lowest_rated. API default: most_relevant.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE'). Defaults to US.
    # Calls POST /travel/airbnb-room.
    def airbnb_room(room, check_in: nil, check_out: nil, adults: nil, children: nil, infants: nil, pets: nil, currency: nil, locale: nil, include_reviews: nil, max_reviews: nil, reviews_sort: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/travel/airbnb-room", {
        room: room,
        check_in: check_in,
        check_out: check_out,
        adults: adults,
        children: children,
        infants: infants,
        pets: pets,
        currency: currency,
        locale: locale,
        include_reviews: include_reviews,
        max_reviews: max_reviews,
        reviews_sort: reviews_sort,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape all reviews of an Airbnb listing.
    #
    # Given an Airbnb `room` (listing id or room URL), returns its guest `reviews`: `id`,
    # `rating`, `text` (as written) with its `language`, `translatedText` (Airbnb's translation
    # into `locale`), `date` (ISO), `localizedDate`, `stayDetail`, `photos`, `reviewer` (`id`,
    # `name`, `photo`, `location` or `tenure`, `profileUrl`, `profileId`, `isSuperhost`,
    # `deleted`) and the host's `response`. `reviewer.id` is Airbnb's account id (the one in its
    # photo URLs; airbnb.com/users/show/<id> asks anonymous visitors to log in), while
    # `profileUrl` is the public profile link Airbnb's own review cards open, keyed by a
    # separate public id given as `profileId`. `deleted` is true when the guest has since closed
    # their Airbnb account; Airbnb still sends the name, photo and location or tenure from the
    # review, and they are returned as sent. Reads every review, not just the few on the room
    # page: page with `page` (or `offset`) and `max_reviews` (up to 500 per call), order with
    # `sort` (most_relevant, most_recent, highest_rated, lowest_rated) and drop older reviews
    # with `since`. Also returns the `listing` summary (title, overall and category `rating`,
    # `ratingDistribution`, `ratingCounts`, `reviewsCount`, `reviewTags`, host) and
    # `totalResults`, `hasMore`, `nextPage`, `nextOffset`. `ratingDistribution` is Airbnb's own
    # list of each star's share of the ratings as a 0-1 fraction (`{"stars": 5, "percentage":
    # 0.957}` = 95.7%), not a count like the other review plugins return; `ratingCounts` gives
    # the number of ratings per star (`{"5": 692, ...}`) worked out from those shares and
    # `reviewsCount`, and is null when the shares do not fit it (seen on hotel listings, whose
    # distribution covers more ratings than `reviewsCount`). Billed one unit per 50-review page
    # read (`pagesFetched`). Companion to the Airbnb search (`/travel/airbnb`) and listing
    # (`/travel/airbnb-room`) plugins.
    #
    # @param room [String] Airbnb listing id (e.g. 23860926) or a room URL on any Airbnb domain,
    # e.g. 'https://www.airbnb.co.uk/rooms/23860926'.
    # @param max_reviews [Integer] How many reviews to return (1-500). Airbnb serves 50 reviews
    # per request; each request is one billed unit. API default: 50.
    # @param page [Integer] 1-based page of `max_reviews`-sized pages (1-1000). Use the
    # `nextPage` of a previous response to continue. API default: 1.
    # @param offset [Integer] Start at this review position instead of `page` (0-20000). Use the
    # `nextOffset` of a previous response to resume exactly.
    # @param sort [String] Review order: most_relevant, most_recent, highest_rated, lowest_rated
    # (the default is Airbnb's own order). API default: most_relevant.
    # @param locale [String] Language to translate reviews and host responses into, e.g. en, de,
    # fr, es, pt-BR. The original text and its language are always returned too. API default:
    # en.
    # @param since [String] Only reviews written on or after this date (YYYY-MM-DD). With
    # sort=most_recent it also stops paging once older reviews start.
    # @param include_listing [Boolean] Also return the listing summary (title, overall and
    # category ratings, rating distribution, host). Set false to skip that request. API default:
    # true.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE').
    # Calls POST /travel/airbnb-reviews.
    def airbnb_reviews(room, max_reviews: nil, page: nil, offset: nil, sort: nil, locale: nil, since: nil, include_listing: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/travel/airbnb-reviews", {
        room: room,
        max_reviews: max_reviews,
        page: page,
        offset: offset,
        sort: sort,
        locale: locale,
        since: since,
        include_listing: include_listing,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape a TripAdvisor list (restaurants / hotels / attractions).
    #
    # Given a TripAdvisor list `url` (Restaurants, Hotels or Attractions for a city/area),
    # returns the places as JSON: `name`, `url`, `image`, `rating`, `reviewCount`, `priceRange`,
    # `cuisine`, `phone`, `address`, `country` and `coordinates`. Paginates through
    # TripAdvisor's `-oaN-` pages up to `max_results`. Data is read from TripAdvisor's
    # schema.org ld+json, which is more stable than its HTML.
    #
    # @param url [String] TripAdvisor list URL (Restaurants / Hotels / Attractions), e.g.
    # https://www.tripadvisor.com/Restaurants-g60763-New_York_City_New_York.html
    # @param max_results [Integer] How many places to collect across pages (~30 per page, max
    # 1020). TripAdvisor renders slowly, so large values take a while. API default: 30.
    # @param page [Integer] 1-based results page to start from. API default: 1.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE'). Leave blank to let the API pick.
    # Calls POST /travel/tripadvisor.
    def tripadvisor_list(url, max_results: nil, page: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/travel/tripadvisor", {
        url: url,
        max_results: max_results,
        page: page,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape TripAdvisor reviews (hotels / restaurants / attractions).
    #
    # Given a TripAdvisor place review `url` (Hotel_Review, Restaurant_Review, Attraction_Review
    # or AttractionProductReview, any TripAdvisor site) or a `location_id`, returns the place
    # summary (`name`, `type`, `rating`, `reviewsCount`, `ratingHistogram`, `subratings`,
    # `address`, `coordinates`, `phone`, `website`) and its `reviews`: `title`, `text`,
    # `rating`, `publishedDate`, `travelDate`, `tripType`, `language`, `helpfulVotes`, `user`,
    # `subratings`, `photos` and `ownerResponse`. Newest first; pages through TripAdvisor's
    # review pages up to `max_reviews`. Pick the review language with `language` and cut off old
    # reviews with `since`. Billed one unit per review page fetched (`pagesFetched`).
    #
    # @param url [String] TripAdvisor place review page on any TripAdvisor site: Hotel_Review,
    # Restaurant_Review, Attraction_Review or AttractionProductReview, e.g.
    # https://www.tripadvisor.com/Hotel_Review-g60763-d93589-Reviews-The_Michelangelo_New_York-
    # New_York_City_New_York.html. Pass this or `location_id`.
    # @param location_id [Integer] TripAdvisor location id (the digits after -d in a place URL,
    # e.g. 93589). Pass this or `url`.
    # @param max_reviews [Integer] How many reviews to collect across pages (1-200). TripAdvisor
    # shows 10 reviews per page (15 for restaurants); each page is one billed unit. API default:
    # 20.
    # @param page [Integer] 1-based review page to start from (10 reviews per page, 15 for
    # restaurants). Use the `nextPage` of a previous response to continue. API default: 1.
    # @param language [String] Review language, which TripAdvisor ties to its site: en, de, fr,
    # it, es, pt, nl, ja, ko, ru, sv, tr, da. Default: the language of the URL's site
    # (tripadvisor.com = en), or - when that site lists no reviews for the place - the place's
    # most-reviewed of these languages. Reviews written in other languages appear machine-
    # translated, flagged by `isMachineTranslated` and `originalLanguage`.
    # @param since [String] Only reviews published on or after this date (YYYY-MM-DD). Stops
    # paging once older reviews start, so it also saves pages.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE'). Leave blank: pinning a country makes the call much slower.
    # Calls POST /travel/tripadvisor-reviews.
    def tripadvisor_reviews(url = nil, location_id: nil, max_reviews: nil, page: nil, language: nil, since: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/travel/tripadvisor-reviews", {
        url: url,
        location_id: location_id,
        max_reviews: max_reviews,
        page: page,
        language: language,
        since: since,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape Google Shopping product listings.
    #
    # Runs the Google Shopping vertical (`udm=28`) for `q` and returns the product cards as
    # JSON: `title`, `price`, `currency`, `currencyCode`, `originalPrice`, `discount`,
    # `merchant`, `rating`, `reviewCount` and Google's own ids for the product (`productId`,
    # `catalogId`, `offerId` - stable across searches, for tracking one product). Set
    # `proxy_country`/`gl` to target a market (prices/merchants are region-specific) and
    # `min_price`/`max_price` to filter. One render returns the first page of products (v1).
    # Note: Google Shopping exposes no per-product URL, so `url` is omitted and `image` is best-
    # effort.
    #
    # @param q [String] Product search term, e.g. 'wireless headphones'.
    # @param gl [String] Market of search, ISO-2, e.g. 'us', 'de'. Sets prices/merchants. API
    # default: us.
    # @param min_price [Integer] Minimum price filter (whole units, gl currency).
    # @param max_price [Integer] Maximum price filter (whole units, gl currency).
    # @param max_results [Integer] How many products to return (v1: first page). API default:
    # 60.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE'). Prices/merchants are market-specific.
    # Calls POST /goods/google-shopping.
    def google_shopping(q, gl: nil, min_price: nil, max_price: nil, max_results: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/goods/google-shopping", {
        q: q,
        gl: gl,
        min_price: min_price,
        max_price: max_price,
        max_results: max_results,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape Google Jobs listings.
    #
    # Runs the Google Jobs vertical (`udm=8`) for `q` and returns the job cards as JSON:
    # `title`, `company`, `location`, `via` (source), `salary`, `employmentType`, `remote`,
    # `postedAge`, `qualifications`, `benefits`, `logo`, a stable `jobId` and a `shareUrl`.
    # Append a `location` to narrow the search, and set `proxy_country`/`gl`/`hl` to target a
    # market. One render returns ~10 jobs (v1).
    #
    # @param q [String] Job search term, e.g. 'software engineer' or 'nurse'.
    # @param location [String] Optional location, appended to the query, e.g. 'Austin, TX'.
    # @param hl [String] Google UI language, e.g. 'en', 'de'. API default: en.
    # @param gl [String] Google country of search, ISO-2, e.g. 'us'. API default: us.
    # @param max_results [Integer] How many jobs to return. One render yields ~10 (v1). API
    # default: 10.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE'). Jobs are location-sensitive; pick the market you want.
    # Calls POST /jobs/google-jobs.
    def google_jobs(q, location: nil, hl: nil, gl: nil, max_results: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/jobs/google-jobs", {
        q: q,
        location: location,
        hl: hl,
        gl: gl,
        max_results: max_results,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape Google Trends (interest over time, by region, related queries).
    #
    # Compares 1-5 `keywords` on Google Trends and returns the data behind the explore page as
    # JSON: `interestOverTime` (one point per time bucket with a 0-100 value per keyword, ISO
    # `date`, `timestamp`, `isPartial`), `averages`, `interestByRegion` (`geoCode`, `geoName`,
    # values per keyword), and per keyword `relatedQueries` and `relatedTopics` (opt-in via
    # `sections`), each with `top` and `rising` lists. Filter with `geo` (worldwide, country,
    # region or US metro), `timeframe` (presets such as 'today 12-m' or a custom date range),
    # `category` and `property` (web, images, news, youtube, shopping). Use `sections` to fetch
    # only what you need. Billed as one request per keyword compared.
    #
    # @param keywords [String, Array<String>] 1-5 search terms to compare, comma-separated, e.g.
    # 'coffee,tea'. A Knowledge Graph topic id such as '/m/0dl567' also works.
    # @param geo [String] Where: empty = worldwide, an ISO-2 country ('US'), a region ('US-CA')
    # or a US metro ('US-CA-807').
    # @param timeframe [String] Time range: now 1-H, now 4-H, now 1-d, now 7-d, today 1-m, today
    # 3-m, today 12-m, today 5-y, all, or a custom 'YYYY-MM-DD YYYY-MM-DD' (hourly for the last
    # 7 days: 'YYYY-MM-DDTHH YYYY-MM-DDTHH'). API default: today 12-m.
    # @param category [Integer] Google Trends category id (0 = all categories, e.g. 71 = Food &
    # Drink, 5 = Computers & Electronics). API default: 0.
    # @param property [String] Which Google search to measure: web, images, news, youtube,
    # shopping. API default: web.
    # @param hl [String] Language of labels (region names, topic titles), e.g. 'en-US', 'de',
    # 'fr'. API default: en-US.
    # @param tz [Integer] Timezone offset in minutes that day/hour buckets align to (Trends'
    # convention: 300 = UTC-5, -60 = UTC+1). Default 0 = UTC. API default: 0.
    # @param region_resolution [String] Level of the interest-by-region map: country, region,
    # city, dma. Default = Trends' own choice (countries worldwide, regions in a country).
    # @param include_low_volume [Boolean] Include regions with low search volume in the region
    # map. API default: false.
    # @param sections [String] What to fetch: a comma list of interest_over_time,
    # interest_by_region, related_queries, related_topics, or 'all'. Fewer sections = faster.
    # related_topics is opt-in (Google often returns it empty). API default:
    # interest_over_time,interest_by_region,related_queries.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE'). Optional - by default a country outside the US and Europe is used; results follow
    # `geo`, not the proxy country.
    # Calls POST /trends/google-trends.
    def google_trends(keywords, geo: nil, timeframe: nil, category: nil, property: nil, hl: nil, tz: nil, region_resolution: nil, include_low_volume: nil, sections: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/trends/google-trends", {
        keywords: plugin_join(keywords, ","),
        geo: geo,
        timeframe: timeframe,
        category: category,
        property: property,
        hl: hl,
        tz: tz,
        region_resolution: region_resolution,
        include_low_volume: include_low_volume,
        sections: sections,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape Google Maps place details.
    #
    # Returns the full data for one Google Maps place as JSON: `name`, `address` (+
    # `addressLines`), `phone`, `website`, `rating`, `categories`, `latitude`/`longitude`,
    # `plusCode`, `hours` + `openNow`, `description`, `photo`, `cid`, `featureId`,
    # `googleMapsUrl` and `relatedPlaces`. Identify the place with `place_id` (ChIJ...), a Maps
    # `url`, or a raw `feature_id` (0xHEX:0xHEX). Location-neutral - the feature id resolves the
    # place directly; `proxy_country` defaults to a US proxy to clear Google's consent wall.
    #
    # @param url [String] A Google Maps place URL (share link or /maps/place/... URL). The
    # feature id or place_id is read out of it.
    # @param place_id [String] A Google place_id, e.g. 'ChIJN1t_tDeuEmsRUsoyG83frY4'.
    # @param feature_id [String] A Maps feature id '0xHEX:0xHEX', e.g.
    # '0x47e66e2964e34e2d:0x8ddca9ee380ef7e0'. Fastest - no resolve step.
    # @param hl [String] Google UI language, e.g. 'en', 'de', 'fr'. API default: en.
    # @param gl [String] Google country, ISO-2 lowercase, e.g. 'us'. API default: us.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE'). Defaults to US - an EU country hits Google's consent wall. API default: us.
    # @param raw [Boolean] Include the decoded place core array under `raw`. API default: false.
    # Calls POST /maps/place-details.
    def maps_place_details(url: nil, place_id: nil, feature_id: nil, hl: nil, gl: nil, proxy_country: nil, raw: nil)
      post_json("/maps/place-details", {
        url: url,
        place_id: place_id,
        feature_id: feature_id,
        hl: hl,
        gl: gl,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
        raw: raw,
      })
    end

    # Scrape Google Hotels search results (prices, ratings, amenities).
    #
    # Runs a Google Hotels search for `q` and returns the properties as JSON: `name`, `type`
    # (hotel / vacation_rental), `propertyToken`, `price` for the stay (`perNight`,
    # `perNightWithTaxes`, `total`, `totalWithTaxes`, `taxes`, Google's `display` string),
    # `deal`, `rating`, `reviews`, `ratingsBreakdown`, `reviewTopics`, `hotelClass`, `stars`,
    # `amenities`, `description`, `website`, `latitude`/`longitude`, `nearbyPlaces`,
    # `thumbnail`, `images` and `mapsUrl`, plus the page's sponsored `ads`. A query naming one
    # hotel returns just that hotel (`matchedProperty`). Set the stay with
    # `check_in`/`check_out`, the party with `adults` and `children` (ages), and `currency`,
    # `hl` (language) and `gl` (market). `applied` echoes the dates, currency and place Google
    # actually used. Collect up to `max_results` across pages (each Google page is billed as one
    # request, see `pagesFetched`); `nextPageToken` continues the list. Pass a `propertyToken`
    # to `/travel/google-hotels-property` for every booking site's price.
    #
    # @param q [String] What to search, as on Google Hotels: a place ('hotels in Paris',
    # 'Lisbon'), an area or landmark, or a hotel name.
    # @param check_in [String] Check-in date, YYYY-MM-DD. Leave check_in and check_out empty for
    # Google's default stay.
    # @param check_out [String] Check-out date, YYYY-MM-DD (stays of up to 30 nights).
    # @param adults [Integer] Number of adults (1-10). API default: 2.
    # @param children [String] Ages of the children, comma-separated, e.g. '7,10' (up to 6).
    # @param currency [String] Price currency, ISO 4217 (USD, EUR, GBP, ...). API default: USD.
    # @param hl [String] Language of names, amenities and price formats, e.g. 'en', 'de', 'fr'.
    # API default: en.
    # @param gl [String] Market (2-letter country). Google localizes the booking sites to it.
    # API default: us.
    # @param page_token [String] `nextPageToken` of a previous response, to continue where it
    # stopped.
    # @param max_results [Integer] Properties to collect (1-100). Google serves about 20 per
    # page; each page is billed as one request. API default: 20.
    # Calls POST /travel/google-hotels.
    def google_hotels(q, check_in: nil, check_out: nil, adults: nil, children: nil, currency: nil, hl: nil, gl: nil, page_token: nil, max_results: nil)
      post_json("/travel/google-hotels", {
        q: q,
        check_in: check_in,
        check_out: check_out,
        adults: adults,
        children: children,
        currency: currency,
        hl: hl,
        gl: gl,
        page_token: page_token,
        max_results: max_results,
      })
    end

    # Scrape one Google Hotels property: prices from every booking site.
    #
    # Returns one property from Google Hotels as JSON, identified by the `property_token` of a
    # `/travel/google-hotels` result: `offers` from every booking site Google lists for the stay
    # (`provider`, `official` for the hotel's own site, `perNight`/`perNightWithTaxes`,
    # `total`/`totalWithTaxes`, a Google `link` to book), `rooms` with room-level rates and
    # free-cancellation deadlines where Google shows them, the lowest `price`, `address`,
    # `phone`, `website`, `description` and `about`, `rating`, `reviews`, `ratingsBreakdown`,
    # `reviewTopics`, `hotelClass`, `latitude`/`longitude`, `nearbyPlaces`, `images`, `placeId`
    # and `mapsUrl`. Set the stay with `check_in`/`check_out`, the party with
    # `adults`/`children`, and `currency`, `hl`, `gl`. Billed as one request.
    #
    # @param property_token [String] `propertyToken` of a /travel/google-hotels result.
    # @param check_in [String] Check-in date, YYYY-MM-DD. Leave check_in and check_out empty for
    # Google's default stay.
    # @param check_out [String] Check-out date, YYYY-MM-DD (stays of up to 30 nights).
    # @param adults [Integer] Number of adults (1-10). API default: 2.
    # @param children [String] Ages of the children, comma-separated, e.g. '7,10' (up to 6).
    # @param currency [String] Price currency, ISO 4217 (USD, EUR, GBP, ...). API default: USD.
    # @param hl [String] Language, e.g. 'en', 'de', 'fr'. API default: en.
    # @param gl [String] Market (2-letter country). Google localizes the booking sites to it.
    # API default: us.
    # Calls POST /travel/google-hotels-property.
    def google_hotels_property(property_token, check_in: nil, check_out: nil, adults: nil, children: nil, currency: nil, hl: nil, gl: nil)
      post_json("/travel/google-hotels-property", {
        property_token: property_token,
        check_in: check_in,
        check_out: check_out,
        adults: adults,
        children: children,
        currency: currency,
        hl: hl,
        gl: gl,
      })
    end

    # Scrape Google News (search or section).
    #
    # Searches Google News for `q`, or lists a section with `topic` (world, nation, business,
    # technology, entertainment, sports, science, health, top, or a topic id), and returns
    # `articles`: `position`, `title`, `url` (the publisher's article URL), `source`,
    # `sourceUrl`, `publishedAt` (ISO 8601 UTC), `imageUrl` (+ `imageWidth`/`imageHeight`),
    # `authors`, `googleNewsId` and `googleNewsUrl`. Metadata only - no article text. Narrow a
    # search with `when` (e.g. '1d') or `after`/`before` dates; pick the edition with `hl` and
    # `gl`. Google News answers a search with up to about 100 articles (no paging - use date
    # windows to go further back). One unit per call.
    #
    # @param q [String] Search query, e.g. 'tesla' or '"web scraping"'. Google News operators
    # work: quotes, OR, -word, site:reuters.com, intitle:. Pass either `q` or `topic`.
    # @param topic [String] A section instead of a search: world, nation, business, technology,
    # entertainment, sports, science, health, or top (the edition's headlines). A Google News
    # topic id (the 'CAAq...' part of a news.google.com/topics/ URL) also works.
    # @param hl [String] Language of the Google News edition, e.g. 'en', 'en-GB', 'de', 'fr',
    # 'pt-BR'. API default: en.
    # @param gl [String] Country of the Google News edition (2-letter code), e.g. 'us', 'gb',
    # 'de'. API default: us.
    # @param when [String] Only articles from the last period: '1h' to '720h', '1d', '7d', '2w',
    # '1m', '1y'. Search (`q`) only.
    # @param after [String] Only articles published after this date (YYYY-MM-DD). Search only.
    # @param before [String] Only articles published before this date (YYYY-MM-DD). Search only.
    # @param max_results [Integer] Most articles to return, 1-250. Google News returns up to
    # about 100 articles for a search and about 250 for a section; one call is billed one unit
    # whatever the count. API default: 100.
    # Calls POST /search/google-news.
    def google_news(q = nil, topic: nil, hl: nil, gl: nil, when: nil, after: nil, before: nil, max_results: nil)
      post_json("/search/google-news", {
        q: q,
        topic: topic,
        hl: hl,
        gl: gl,
        when: binding.local_variable_get(:when),
        after: after,
        before: before,
        max_results: max_results,
      })
    end

    # Scrape Google ads (Ads Transparency Center).
    #
    # Returns an advertiser's Google ads (Search, YouTube, Display, Shopping, Maps, Play) from
    # the Google Ads Transparency Center for competitor analysis: per ad `creativeId`,
    # `advertiserId`/`advertiserName`, `format` (text/image/video), `firstShown`, `lastShown`,
    # `daysShown`, `matchedDomain`, the preview (`imageUrl` with size, or `previewUrl`),
    # `googleCreativeId`, `googleCustomerId`, `adGroupId` and `adUrl`. Pick ads by `advertiser`
    # (name or id) or `domain`; filter with `region`, `platform`, `format` and
    # `date_from`/`date_to`. `include_details` adds per-region reach and targeting,
    # `include_preview` the ad's texts and YouTube video. Collects up to `max_results` (continue
    # with `page_token`); billed one unit per page of up to 100 ads, plus one per detail and per
    # preview fetched (`callUnits`).
    #
    # @param advertiser [String] Advertiser to pull ads for: a name (e.g. 'Nike') - resolved to
    # the best-matching advertiser, reported in `advertisersUsed` (a single brand name no
    # advertiser is named like falls back to its domain) - or an advertiser id
    # ('AR16735076323512287233'). Comma-separate up to 10. Pass `advertiser` or `domain`.
    # @param domain [String] Website the ads point to, e.g. 'nike.com' (ads of every advertiser
    # for that domain).
    # @param region [String] Where the ads were shown: a 2-letter country code ('US', 'DE'), a
    # comma list of them, or 'anywhere' (default).
    # @param platform [String] Only ads shown on these platforms (comma list): search, youtube,
    # maps, play, shopping.
    # @param format [String] Only one ad format: text, image or video.
    # @param date_from [String] Only ads shown on or after this date (YYYY-MM-DD).
    # @param date_to [String] Only ads shown on or before this date (YYYY-MM-DD).
    # @param sort [String] Order of the ads: 'last_shown' (most recently shown first). API
    # default: last_shown.
    # @param max_results [Integer] Ads to collect, 1-1000. Pages of up to 100 are fetched as
    # needed; each page is billed one unit. API default: 100.
    # @param page_token [String] Continue a previous call: its `nextPageToken`.
    # @param include_details [Boolean] Add `details` per ad: regions shown with first/last date
    # (plus impressions per platform for EU regions), EU reach, disclosed targeting and all
    # preview variations. One unit per ad, for up to 300 ads per call. API default: false.
    # @param include_preview [Boolean] Add `preview` per ad: what it shows - headline,
    # description and display URL of text ads, product title and merchant of shopping ads, image
    # URLs, the YouTube video of video ads. One unit per ad that has a preview script, for up to
    # 40 ads per call. Search text ads often exist only as an image (`imageUrl`). API default:
    # false.
    # Calls POST /ads/google-ads-transparency.
    def google_ads_transparency(advertiser: nil, domain: nil, region: nil, platform: nil, format: nil, date_from: nil, date_to: nil, sort: nil, max_results: nil, page_token: nil, include_details: nil, include_preview: nil)
      post_json("/ads/google-ads-transparency", {
        advertiser: advertiser,
        domain: domain,
        region: region,
        platform: platform,
        format: format,
        date_from: date_from,
        date_to: date_to,
        sort: sort,
        max_results: max_results,
        page_token: page_token,
        include_details: include_details,
        include_preview: include_preview,
      })
    end

    # Look up Google advertisers (Ads Transparency Center).
    #
    # Finds advertisers on the Google Ads Transparency Center by name: `advertisers`
    # (`advertiserId` to use with /ads/google-ads-transparency, `name`, `country`,
    # `creativesMin`/`creativesMax` - how many ads they ran - and `verified`) and matching
    # website `domains`. One unit per call.
    #
    # @param q [String] Advertiser name or brand to look up, e.g. 'nike'.
    # @param region [String] Count ads shown in these regions only: a 2-letter country code, a
