    # Scrape Glassdoor company reviews.
    #
    # Returns a company's Glassdoor employee reviews as JSON. Each review has `id`, `url`,
    # `title`, `rating` (1-5), `subratings` (work/life balance, culture and values, diversity
    # and inclusion, career opportunities, compensation and benefits, senior management),
    # `recommendToFriend`, `ceoApproval`, `businessOutlook`, `pros`, `cons`, `advice`,
    # `jobTitle`, `employmentStatus`, `isCurrentEmployee`, `yearsOfEmployment`, `location`,
    # `date`, `helpfulCount`, `notHelpfulCount`, `featured`, `employerResponse` and `view` (the
    # sort / filter view it came from). The response also carries `summary` (overall rating,
    # review counts, recommend / CEO approval / business outlook percentages, the CEO, sub-
    # rating averages, star distribution, reviews in the last 7 and 30 days), `companyMeta`
    # (name, logo, website, industry, sector, headquarters, size) and `totalReviews`. The counts
    # are Glassdoor's own and measure different sets: `totalReviews` is the number of reviews
    # matching every filter of the call (`rating`, `job_title`, `keyword`, `topic`,
    # `employment_status`, `language`, `current_only`; the default view is English reviews by
    # full-time and part-time employees), the set the views read from. `summary.allReviewsCount`
    # is every review Glassdoor holds for the employer, rated or not, in any language and
    # employment status; no filter changes it. `summary.reviewCount` is the number of ratings
    # behind `summary.overallRating`, and `summary.ratedReviewsCount` the number of reviews that
    # carry a star rating; Glassdoor counts the two separately, so they can differ by a few.
    # Both follow `rating` but not the language or employment filters, so they are usually above
    # `totalReviews`. `summary.ratingDistribution` holds the star counts Glassdoor sends for its
    # rating bars (`overall`, each sub-rating, and `recommendToFriend`); for employers with many
    # reviews they cover only part of them (Google: 18,643 overall against a `reviewCount` of
    # 52,845), so read them as proportions, not totals, and with `rating` set only that star is
    # counted. `summary.reviewsLast7Days` and `reviewsLast30Days` count reviews matching the
    # filters. Give `company` as a Glassdoor employer id, a Glassdoor company URL or a company
    # name. Only US proxies are used (`proxy_country` accepts US or blank): Glassdoor redirects
    # other countries to their local site, where these pages do not exist. Glassdoor shows
    # anonymous visitors only the first page (3 reviews) of each sort / filter view and
    # redirects deeper pages to sign-in, so there is no page parameter: use `sort`, `rating` and
    # the filters to reach other reviews, or raise `max_results` to read several views in one
    # call. `hasMore` is always false and `nextPage` null; `publicLimitReached` is true when
    # fewer reviews came back than asked because the views anonymous visitors may read ran out.
    # Every field is always present and is `null` when Glassdoor does not show it. Billed per
    # review view fetched (`pagesFetched`).
    #
    # @param company [String] The company: a Glassdoor employer id (e.g. '9079'), any Glassdoor
    # company URL (e.g. 'https://www.glassdoor.com/Reviews/Google-Reviews-E9079.htm'), or a
    # company name (e.g. 'Bank of America', matched with Glassdoor's company search).
    # @param sort [String] Review order: recent, helpful, highest, lowest. API default: recent.
    # @param rating [Integer] Only reviews with this overall star rating (1-5).
    # @param job_title [String] Only reviews from job titles matching this text, e.g. 'software
    # engineer' (Glassdoor's own job-title search).
    # @param keyword [String] Only reviews mentioning this keyword, e.g. 'remote'.
    # @param topic [String] Only reviews about one topic: compensation, culture,
    # career_development, management, work_life_balance, diversity_and_inclusion.
    # @param employment_status [String] Comma list of full_time, part_time, contract, intern,
    # freelance. Defaults to Glassdoor's own view: full_time,part_time.
    # @param language [String] Comma list of review languages (ISO 639-3): eng, fra, deu, nld,
    # por, spa, ita. Defaults to eng.
    # @param current_only [Boolean] Only reviews by current employees. API default: false.
    # @param max_results [Integer] How many reviews to return. Glassdoor shows anonymous
    # visitors 3 reviews per view; above that the call also reads the other sort orders and each
    # star rating, and merges the distinct reviews. API default: 3.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from. Only US (or blank,
    # the default) is accepted: Glassdoor redirects visitors from other countries to their local
    # site, where the English reviews pages do not exist.
    # Calls POST /reviews/glassdoor.
    def glassdoor_reviews(company, sort: nil, rating: nil, job_title: nil, keyword: nil, topic: nil, employment_status: nil, language: nil, current_only: nil, max_results: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/reviews/glassdoor", {
        company: company,
        sort: sort,
        rating: rating,
        job_title: job_title,
        keyword: keyword,
        topic: topic,
        employment_status: employment_status,
        language: language,
        current_only: current_only,
        max_results: max_results,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

