    # Scrape company career sites (Greenhouse, Lever, Ashby, Workday and more).
    #
    # Every open job on a company's career site hosted on Greenhouse, Lever, Ashby, Workday,
    # SmartRecruiters, Workable or Recruitee, from its career page `url` (or `provider` +
    # `company`). Each job (`jobs`) has `id`, `title`, `company`, `provider`, `url`, `applyUrl`,
    # `location` and `locations`, `country`, `remote`, `workplaceType` (remote / hybrid /
    # onsite), `department`, `team`, `employmentType`, `postedAt`, `updatedAt`, `requisitionId`,
    # `salary` and `salaryRanges` (min, max, currency, interval) where the ATS publishes pay,
    # `customFields` (the ATS's extra fields) and, with `include_description=true`,
    # `descriptionText` and `descriptionHtml`. A field the ATS does not expose is null. Filter
    # with `keyword` (title words), `location`, `department` and `remote`; `limit` caps the jobs
    # returned. The answer also carries the detected `provider`, `company`, `companyName`,
    # `boardUrl`, `totalJobs` on the board and `matchedJobs`. A link to one posting returns just
    # that posting with its description. Billed one unit per upstream request that returned data
    # (`pagesFetched`): one for a whole Greenhouse, Ashby, Workable or Recruitee board, one per
    # Lever (100 jobs), Workday (20 jobs) or SmartRecruiters (100 jobs) listing page, plus one
    # per Workday / SmartRecruiters posting whose description is read.
    #
    # @param url [String] The company's career page on its ATS, e.g. 'https://job-
    # boards.greenhouse.io/stripe', 'https://jobs.lever.co/palantir',
    # 'https://jobs.ashbyhq.com/ramp',
    # 'https://nvidia.wd5.myworkdayjobs.com/NVIDIAExternalCareerSite',
    # 'https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/Ubisoft2', 'https://apply.workable.com/huggingface',
    # 'https://bunq.recruitee.com'. A link to one posting returns just that posting.
    # @param provider [String] Instead of `url`: the ATS (greenhouse, lever, ashby, workday,
    # smartrecruiters, workable, recruitee), with `company`.
    # @param company [String] Instead of `url`: the company's board name on that ATS, as in its
    # career URL: Greenhouse token ('stripe'), Lever / Ashby / Workable / Recruitee slug,
    # SmartRecruiters company id ('Ubisoft2'), Workday '<tenant>.wd<N>/<site>'
    # ('nvidia.wd5/NVIDIAExternalCareerSite').
    # @param keyword [String] Only jobs whose title contains every word, e.g. 'software
    # engineer'.
    # @param location [String] Only jobs in this place (city, region or country as the company
    # writes it, e.g. 'London', 'Germany', 'Remote'); case-insensitive.
    # @param department [String] Only jobs in this department or team, e.g. 'Engineering'.
    # @param remote [Boolean] Only remote jobs. API default: false.
    # @param limit [Integer] How many jobs to return (1-2000). API default: 100.
    # @param include_description [Boolean] Add each job's full description (`descriptionText`,
    # `descriptionHtml`). On Workday and SmartRecruiters this reads every posting separately
    # (one billed request each, at most 300 per call). API default: false.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the requests are sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE'). Leave blank to let the API pick.
    # Calls POST /jobs/career-site.
    def career_site_jobs(url = nil, provider: nil, company: nil, keyword: nil, location: nil, department: nil, remote: nil, limit: nil, include_description: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/jobs/career-site", {
        url: url,
        provider: provider,
        company: company,
        keyword: keyword,
        location: location,
        department: department,
        remote: remote,
        limit: limit,
        include_description: include_description,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape Upwork job posts.
    #
    # Searches Upwork jobs for `q` (or lists the newest jobs without it) and returns `jobs`:
    # `id`, `ciphertext`, `url`, `title`, `description` (full text), `publishedAt`, `createdAt`,
    # `renewedAt`, `jobType` (hourly / fixed), `hourlyMin`, `hourlyMax`, `budget` (fixed price),
    # `currency`, `experienceLevel`, `duration`, `workload`, `skills`, plus `totalResults` and
    # `nextStart`. Filter with `job_type`, `experience`, `budget`, `hourly_rate`,
    # `client_hires`, `workload`, `duration`, `location`, `category`, `contract_to_hire`; sort
    # by `relevance` or `recency`. `details=true` adds, per job: `proposals`, `interviewing`,
    # `invitesSent`, `hired`, `positionsToHire`, `lastClientActivity`, `category`,
    # `qualifications` and `client` (`country`, `city`, `timezone`, `paymentVerified`, `rating`,
    # `reviews`, `totalSpent`, `hires`, `memberSince`; no client names). A private listing is
    # marked `privateListing: true` without those fields. Upwork shows signed-out visitors the
    # first 5000 results of a search. Billing: one unit per results page of up to 50 jobs, plus
    # one per job page fetched with `details=true` (`callUnits`).
    #
    # @param q [String] What to search for, e.g. 'python' or 'logo design'. Leave empty with
    # sort=recency for the newest jobs.
    # @param job_type [String] hourly or fixed.
    # @param experience [String] Experience level, comma list of: entry, intermediate, expert.
    # @param budget [String] Fixed-price budget in USD, comma list of ranges like '100-499',
    # '1000-4999' or '5000-'.
    # @param hourly_rate [String] Hourly rate in USD, e.g. '25-50' or '40-'.
    # @param client_hires [String] Client's past hires, comma list of: 0, 1-9, 10-.
    # @param workload [String] part_time (under 30 hrs/week) or full_time.
    # @param duration [String] Project length, comma list of: week, month, semester, ongoing
    # (under a month, 1-3 months, 3-6 months, over 6 months).
    # @param location [String] Client location, comma list of regions or countries as Upwork
    # names them, e.g. 'Europe' or 'United States'.
    # @param category [String] Job category, comma list of names (Accounting & Consulting, Admin
    # Support, Customer Service, Data Science & Analytics, Design & Creative, Engineering &
    # Architecture, It & Networking, Legal, Sales & Marketing, Translation, Web, Mobile &
    # Software Dev, Writing) or category ids.
    # @param contract_to_hire [Boolean] Only contract-to-hire jobs. API default: false.
    # @param sort [String] relevance (default with q) or recency (default without q).
    # @param start [Integer] 0-based position of the first job, 0-4999 (Upwork shows signed-out
    # visitors the first 5000 results); `nextStart` continues. API default: 0.
    # @param max_results [Integer] Jobs to collect, 1-5000. Each results page (up to 50 jobs) is
    # billed one unit. API default: 50.
    # @param details [Boolean] Add each job's page: proposals, client stats, qualifications. Up
    # to 100 jobs; one more unit per job page fetched. API default: false.
    # Calls POST /jobs/upwork.
    def upwork_jobs(q = nil, job_type: nil, experience: nil, budget: nil, hourly_rate: nil, client_hires: nil, workload: nil, duration: nil, location: nil, category: nil, contract_to_hire: nil, sort: nil, start: nil, max_results: nil, details: nil)
      post_json("/jobs/upwork", {
        q: q,
        job_type: job_type,
        experience: experience,
        budget: budget,
        hourly_rate: hourly_rate,
        client_hires: client_hires,
        workload: workload,
        duration: duration,
        location: location,
        category: category,
        contract_to_hire: contract_to_hire,
        sort: sort,
        start: start,
        max_results: max_results,
        details: details,
      })
    end

    # Scrape Trustpilot company reviews.
    #
    # Returns a company's Trustpilot profile and reviews as JSON. `company` is the reviewed
    # domain (e.g. `www.nike.com`). The response has a `company` summary (`trustScore`,
    # `totalReviews`, `ratingDistribution` 1-5, `categories`, `contact`) and a `reviews` array -
    # each with `rating`, `title`, `text`, `verified`, `author` (name, country, review count),
    # `reply` (the company's response) and dates. Page with `page`/`pages` (20 reviews per
    # page), and filter with `stars`, `sort` (recency/relevancy), `language`, `date`, `search`,
    # `verified` or `replies`.
    #
    # @param company [String] The reviewed company's domain, e.g. 'www.nike.com' or 'nike.com'.
    # A full site URL or a pasted Trustpilot review URL is also accepted.
    # @param page [Integer] 1-based page to start from (20 reviews/page). API default: 1.
    # @param pages [Integer] How many consecutive pages to fetch from `page` onward (20 reviews
    # each). Stops early at the last page. API default: 1.
    # @param stars [Array<Integer>] Only reviews with these star ratings (1, 2, 3, 4, 5).
    # Repeatable, e.g. stars=4&stars=5.
    # @param sort [String] Ordering: recency, relevancy. Default 'recency' (newest first). API
    # default: recency.
    # @param language [String] Review language ISO-2 code (e.g. 'en', 'de') or 'all'. Default
    # 'all'. API default: all.
    # @param date [String] Only reviews from a recent window: last30days, last3months,
    # last6months, last12months. Omit for all time.
    # @param search [String] Keyword to search within the company's reviews.
    # @param verified [Boolean] Only verified reviews (Trustpilot's verified badge). API
    # default: false.
    # @param replies [Boolean] Only reviews the company has replied to. API default: false.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE'). Defaults to a country chosen for the site.
    # Calls POST /reviews/trustpilot.
    def trustpilot_reviews(company, page: nil, pages: nil, stars: nil, sort: nil, language: nil, date: nil, search: nil, verified: nil, replies: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/reviews/trustpilot", {
        company: company,
        page: page,
        pages: pages,
        stars: stars,
        sort: sort,
        language: language,
        date: date,
        search: search,
        verified: verified,
        replies: replies,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

    # Scrape G2 software reviews.
    #
    # Given a G2 `product` (slug like `slack`, or a g2.com product URL), returns the product
    # summary (`name`, `rating`, `reviewCount`, `starDistribution`, `categories`, `aiSummary`,
    # `pros`, `cons`, `segments`) and its `reviews`: `title`, `rating`, `date`, `reviewer` (job
    # title, industry, company size), `likeBest`, `dislike`, `problemsSolved`, the full
    # `answers`, badges (`validatedReviewer`, `currentUser`, `incentivized`, `source`) and
    # `vendorResponse`. Pages through G2's review pages up to `max_results` (10 per page, at
    # most 10 pages per view); pick the view with `sort`, `stars` and `search`. `details=true`
    # adds the vendor, description, website and languages. `mode=search&query=` searches G2's
    # product catalog. Billed one unit per page fetched (`pagesFetched`).
    #
    # @param product [String] G2 product slug, e.g. 'slack' or 'hubspot-sales-hub', or a g2.com
    # product URL such as https://www.g2.com/products/slack/reviews. Required unless
    # mode=search.
    # @param page [Integer] 1-based page to start from: 10 reviews per page, at most 10 (G2's
    # limit per sort/filter view); with mode=search 20 products per page, at most 20. Use the
    # `nextPage` of a previous response to continue. API default: 1.
    # @param max_results [Integer] How many reviews to collect across pages (1-100, default 10).
    # With mode=search, how many products (1-100, default 20) - search answers whole pages of
    # 20, so it can return a few more. Each page fetched is one billed unit.
    # @param sort [String] Review order: g2_default, most_recent, most_helpful, highest_rated,
    # lowest_rated. Default: G2's own ranking. API default: g2_default.
    # @param stars [Integer] Only reviews with this star rating (1-5).
    # @param search [String] Only reviews whose answers mention this keyword.
    # @param details [Boolean] Also fetch the product page for its vendor, description, website
    # and languages (one more billed unit). API default: false.
    # @param proxy_country [String] Proxy country the request is sent from (ISO-2, e.g. 'US',
    # 'DE'). Leave blank for the country chosen for the site.
    # Calls POST /reviews/g2.
    def g2_reviews(product, page: nil, max_results: nil, sort: nil, stars: nil, search: nil, details: nil, proxy_country: nil)
      post_json("/reviews/g2", {
        mode: "reviews",
        product: product,
        page: page,
        max_results: max_results,
        sort: sort,
        stars: stars,
        search: search,
        details: details,
        proxy_country: proxy_country,
      })
    end

